{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T05:21:08.834Z","ordinal":325449,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_060b64278a9dfb15016add91c3fb3881a0972ade0607315a0f","status":"completed","call_id":"call_68fbe6197dd54556bf2651588504b32e","name":"exec","input":"const r=await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rg -n \\\"Trust this folder|trusted workspace|folder trust|trust.*workspace|workspace.*trust|approval mode|permission.*review\\\" cli/src cli/test docs | head -120 && find .git/nexus-claims -maxdepth 2 -name owner.json -print0 | xargs -0 rg -n -i \\\"trust|workspace|approval|startup\\\" || true && sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md\",workdir:\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",yield_time_ms:10000,max_output_tokens:6000}); text(r.output)\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1717f-73da-7b13-b06c-cebce8fdbefc","create_time":1792905667.9826946}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T05:21:08.925Z","ordinal":325450,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a1717f-73da-7b13-b06c-cebce8fdbefc","session_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","root_turn_id":"01a1717f-73da-7b13-b06c-cebce8fdbefc","response_id":"resp_060b64278a9dfb15016add91b9656481a09cd2ddfc7e072051","usage":{"input_tokens":85831,"cached_input_tokens":84736,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1402,"reasoning_output_tokens":1192,"total_tokens":87233},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":1927461,"cached_input_tokens":1831168,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":8313,"reasoning_output_tokens":4014,"total_tokens":1935774},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":5928642286,"cached_input_tokens":5865182592,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":16781138,"reasoning_output_tokens":9657421,"total_tokens":5945423424}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T05:21:10.033Z","ordinal":325451,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a1717f-73da-7b13-b06c-cebce8fdbefc","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-8e15f3df-0b78-4443-87cb-a633936f886a","process_id":"21901","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n \"Trust this folder|trusted workspace|folder trust|trust.*workspace|workspace.*trust|approval mode|permission.*review\" cli/src cli/test docs | head -120 && find .git/nexus-claims -maxdepth 2 -name owner.json -print0 | xargs -0 rg -n -i \"trust|workspace|approval|startup\" || true && sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'Trust this folder|trusted workspace|folder trust|trust.*workspace|workspace.*trust|approval mode|permission.*review' cli/src cli/test docs","query":"Trust this folder|trusted workspace|folder trust|trust.*workspace|workspace.*trust|approval mode|permission.*review","path":"cli"},{"type":"search","cmd":"find .git/nexus-claims -maxdepth 2 -name owner.json -print0","query":"owner.json","path":"nexus-claims"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md","name":"CONCURRENCY.md","path":"docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"docs/CLIENT_GUIDE.md:26:The client executes workspace tools and shell commands with the current user's permissions. `--approve` / review mode prompts for edits and shell execution. Automatic approval is convenient but gives the configured model authority to request those actions; review consequential changes and command output yourself. `--read-only` refuses mutating tools and commands. `--sandbox` can use a supported bubblewrap or Docker runtime; availability and guarantees vary by host, so do not treat the option name as proof of isolation. A running shell command cannot always be force-stopped after it has started.\ndocs/NEXUS-CLOUD-PROVIDERS-PLAN.md:55:3. Configuration precedence and project trust: flags, environment, user configuration, workspace configuration, restored sessions. Identify what can currently change a destination or credential source.\ndocs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:497:| **EXT-002** | The discovery or presence of executable extension code within a workspace (e.g., in `.nexus/plugins` or `workspace/plugins`) MUST NOT confer host execution authority. Workspace extensions MUST NOT be executed without authority established by configured policy. | Opening or inspecting an untrusted third-party repository containing JavaScript plugins MUST NOT execute that code automatically. |\ndocs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:776:| **F-04** | Automatic execution of untrusted workspace plugins (Demonstrated) | **EXT-002, EXT-003** | Fully resolved incorporating Operator Decision 2 (policy-based execution authority). |\ndocs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:824:   - *Reconciled Change:* Strengthened by `EXT-002` and `EXT-003` to mandate that discovery/presence in a workspace does not confer execution authority. Execution requires configured policy authority, while preconfigured trust eliminates mandatory interactive prompts, resolving `F-04` and Operator Decision 2.\ndocs/PHASE1_SYSTEM_RECONSTRUCTION.md:88:| Validation | TestRunResult, verifiedWorktreeCompletion boolean, SynthesizerState, tool output, evaluator result. | Discovery success advisory; configured verifier alone authorizes auto merge. TDD/evaluator/manual test have separate result semantics. | No durable validation artifact bound to exact mutation set/workspace/commit. | #11 tests prove configured success/failure behavior, not semantic quality of trusted command. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/README.md:26:| 03 | Untrusted workspace inputs and egress boundary | [SPEC-03](specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | [PLAN-03](plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | M1 | SEC-110, 111, 115, 116 |\ndocs/USER_MANUAL.md:295:- **Workspace Trust**: A workspace's own `.mcp.json` / `mcp.json` servers and `.nexus/plugins/` / `plugins/` modules run code from the workspace, so they are loaded only after you trust it: `nexus trust [path]` (undo with `nexus trust --remove`, list with `nexus trust --list`; recorded in `~/.config/nexus/trusted_workspaces.json`, which the agent's file tools cannot write). Untrusted, they are not loaded and Nexus says so (a notice at start, `nexus doctor`, `nexus mcp`, `nexus plugins`); in `--read-only` mode they are never loaded. Servers and plugins in your home configuration (`~/.config/nexus/`, `~/.nexus/`) are always loaded.\ndocs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:9:| [DEC-01](#dec-01-default-execution-posture) | Default execution posture | Review by default; auto only in trusted workspaces | No (resolved) |\ndocs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:36:| **C (chosen)** | Posture follows workspace trust. **Untrusted workspace → review** for `write_workspace`, `exec`, `egress` and `context_inject`. **Trusted workspace → auto** for `write_workspace` and `exec`; `egress` asks once per origin per session. `--yes` or `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto` force auto. `--sandbox` stays available. | Safe for cloned or unknown repos. Low friction on the operator's own repos after one `nexus trust .`. Reuses the existing trust store, so there is no new concept. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:39:**Rationale.** The threat that matters is content from an untrusted repository or the web. Trust is the existing per-workspace boundary that MCP and plugins already honour. Option C closes SEC-01 and VER-01 on exactly the inputs that cause them, and keeps the daily-driver experience on trusted repos.\ndocs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:172:**Chosen:** off by default; opt-in with `NEXUS_KNOWLEDGE_AUTOEXTRACT=1`, with a visible notice each time a rule is saved. Workspace knowledge is loaded only when the workspace is trusted.\ncli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:11:import { isWorkspaceTrusted, listTrustedWorkspaces, setWorkspaceTrusted, untrustedWorkspaceExtensions } from \"../src/workspace_trust.js\";\ncli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:33:describe(\"workspace trust\", () => {\ncli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:34:  test(\"workspace plugins load only when the workspace is trusted, and never in read-only mode\", async () => {\ncli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:68:      const store = path.join(home, \".config\", \"nexus\", \"trusted_workspaces.json\");\ndocs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:96:| EXT-002 | 15: MCP, Plugin & Extension | The discovery or presence of executable extension code within a workspace (e.g., in `.nexus/plugins` or `workspace/plugins`) MUST NOT confer host execution authority. Workspace extensions MUST NOT be executed without authority established by configured policy. | Opening or inspecting an untrusted third-party repository containing JavaScript plugins MUST NOT execute that code automatically. | Phase 2 §19 | `cli/src/plugins.ts, cli/src/mcp.ts`; cli/test/harness.test.ts | SATISFIED_BY_INTEGRATED_SYSTEM | None | Satisfied |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:5:| Status | Ready. Default posture resolved by [DEC-01](../DECISIONS.md#dec-01-default-execution-posture) (option C: review in untrusted workspaces, auto in trusted ones). |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:33:**Verifier runs bypass the gate entirely (VER-01).** Before the first edit (change baseline, `agent.ts:1602-1617`) and at every `task_completed` (`agent.ts:3432-3436`), Nexus runs the repository's own test command (`make test` / `npm test` / `cargo test` / `pytest`, chosen by `tester.ts:113-136`) through `bridge.executeTool` directly. These calls skip `dispatchToolCall`, so even review mode never asks, and no workspace-trust check applies. Opening a hostile repo and approving one `edit_file` therefore runs that repo's arbitrary test script on the host. `/test` and `nexus eval` run `execSync` on the host, bypassing the sandbox and blocking the event loop (VER-14).\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:58:5. **REQ-TUI-102.** At session start, both surfaces state the active posture, how to change it, and whether the workspace is trusted.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:62:   - In an untrusted workspace, the first verifier run requires a one-time approval. The approval shows the exact command and the script body it resolves to (for example the `scripts.test` entry, or the Makefile `test:` recipe).\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:63:   - In a trusted workspace, they run without asking under the auto posture.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:96:| `exec` | run_command, run_tests, LSP server spawn, plugin and MCP tool calls | refused, except the confined read-only allowlist | ask | allowed if the workspace is trusted; otherwise ask (DEC-01 option C) |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:133:Line 1 of the TUI header and the REPL banner: `mode: review · sandbox: off · workspace: untrusted (nexus trust .)`. `nexus doctor` reports the same three facts.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:140:- Persisted sessions do **not** persist an approval mode. Resume uses the current launch posture. This avoids resurrecting authority, per PERS-004.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:144:- Changing the default for untrusted workspaces from `auto` to review (DEC-01 option C) is a user-visible change. Release notes and the startup banner must state it. Provide `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto` and `nexus trust .` + `--yes` for scripted users.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:168:| ACC-SAFE-01 | Fresh HOME, `nexus` (TUI) and `nexus repl`, mock gateway scripted to call `run_command \"echo x > ../outside\"`: in an untrusted workspace the call is not executed without an approval panel. After `nexus trust .` it runs without a panel (DEC-01 option C). | PTY |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:181:- **DEC-01** is resolved as option C (review in untrusted workspaces, auto in trusted ones). Nothing in this spec is blocked.\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:42:| 5 | Route verifier executions through the policy as `exec`. When not trusted and no prior approval exists, emit the approval with `kind: \"verifier\"`, the command, and the resolved script body (read `package.json` `scripts.test`, the Makefile `test:` recipe, `Cargo.toml`/`pytest.ini` presence). Cache the approval per (workspace, command, script digest) for the session. | `agent.ts:1602-1630,3432-3436,4034`; `tester.ts:113-136` (add `describeVerifier()`); `approval_review.ts` (new kind) | 3 |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:1:# SPEC-03: Untrusted workspace inputs and the egress boundary\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:14:Nexus's threat model, as its docs state it, is that the operator's own configuration (`~/.config/nexus`) holds authority. An opened workspace is data until the operator trusts it (USER_MANUAL §4; `cli/src/workspace_trust.ts`). Workspace MCP configs and plugins honour this: they need `nexus trust` and never run in `--read-only`. Several other inputs that come from the workspace or the network do not:\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:42:4. **REQ-SEC-113.** Workspace skills and workspace `knowledge.json` are loaded only for trusted workspaces. In an untrusted workspace they are listed by `/skills` as \"untrusted (not loaded)\", and `activate_skill` on one is refused with the `nexus trust` hint.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:46:6. **REQ-SEC-115.** Nexus loads plugins only from its own documented directories (`~/.config/nexus/plugins`, `~/.nexus/plugins`, and trusted workspace dirs). `~/.config/opencode/plugins` is removed.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:87:In `skills.ts:31-33`, split skill roots into home roots (always) and workspace roots (only if `isWorkspaceTrusted(ws, readOnly)`). In `knowledge.ts`, apply the same gating for loading, make the constructor lazy (no `mkdir`), and add an opt-in flag for `autoExtract`. The model-facing system prompt marks injected skill and knowledge content with its source path and trust state. This is a partial answer to CTX-001 (provenance per injected item) and is recorded in DEC-04.\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:1:# PLAN-03: Untrusted workspace inputs and the egress boundary\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:33:| 6 | Trust-gate workspace skill roots and workspace `knowledge.json`. `/skills` lists untrusted entries as such. `activate_skill` on an untrusted skill is refused with a hint. Injected blocks are annotated with source and trust state (partial CTX-001). | `skills.ts:31-33`; `knowledge.ts`; `agent.ts:3301,3789`; `slash/extension_commands.ts` | — |\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:56:- **`workspace_trust.test.ts`**: extend for skills and knowledge (ACC-TRUST-04/05).\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:86:| Q4 | Enforces trustworthy execution boundaries | every mutating/egress capability gated by mode and context; untrusted workspaces cannot execute code; secrets never leave or persist in plaintext |\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:101:| Q4 boundaries | **FAILS** | read-only mode sandboxed; Rust path canonicalisation; TLS never disabled; failover never to a less-trusted endpoint | review-mode bypasses (T-01/T5, T-02/T14, T-06/T11); workspace code execution (T-03); secrets (T-05, T-07, T-08, T10); listing outside workspace (T15); dry-run executes (T13); TOCTOU (T-11); network tools ungated (T-12) |\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:183:| T-03 | P0 | REPRODUCED | Workspace `plugins/`, `.nexus/plugins/` imported and workspace `.mcp.json` servers started with the full environment, no trust prompt, under `--read-only --approve` | `cli/src/plugins.ts:46-52`; `cli/src/mcp.ts:479-484` |\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:257:| B2 | **Workspace trust**: never import workspace plugins or start workspace MCP servers until the operator trusts that workspace (per-path trust record); `--read-only` disables both; MCP children get an environment allowlist | T-03 | clone a repo containing `plugins/x.mjs` and `.mcp.json` that touch a sentinel → no sentinel without explicit trust, in every mode |\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:294:| 1 | B1 execution authority + B2 workspace trust (+ R4 listing confinement, T13 dry-run) | 0 | capability × mode × context matrix complete and enforced; adversarial suite blocked; no regression in existing suites |\ndocs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:64:| REQ-AGT-010 | CTX-001 (Issue #12 blocker) | Injected context carries provenance and classification metadata. | `agent.ts:3301,3789` | SRC; TEST (v1 subset: `cli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts` ACC-TRUST-04, 5/5 at 2c70d39, source unchanged through 19dedc6) | partial | Y (v1 subset only: ACC-TRUST-04 skills/knowledge source annotations independently accepted 2026-10-18; not the full provenance graph) | DEC-04: v1 scope = skills, knowledge and other injected items carry source path and trust state (REQ-SEC-113); full provenance graph post-v1. Not an installed-RC acceptance. | ACC-TRUST-04 | RR (partial), P (rest) | SPEC-03 |\ndocs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:69:| REQ-SEC-002 | EXT-002, TOP_TIER B2 | Workspace MCP configs and plugins load only after `nexus trust`, and never in `--read-only`. | `workspace_trust.ts`; `mcp.ts:509-531`; `plugins.ts:47-76` | TEST + SRC | tested | N | Skills and knowledge are not gated (→ REQ-SEC-113) | ACC-TRUST-04 | K | SPEC-03 |\ndocs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:119:| REQ-SEC-101 | Audit; DEC-01 | Without opt-in, no model-initiated exec or egress happens without a decision. Untrusted workspace → review; trusted → auto. | safety | `config.ts`, `sidecar.ts:935`, `repl.ts:69`, `index.ts:93,115` | TEST `approval_policy.test.ts`, `tui_surface_e2e.test.ts`, `repl_approval_e2e.test.ts` | tested | ACC-SAFE-01 | **B** | SEC-01, TUI-07 | SPEC-01 / PLAN-01 |\ndocs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:135:| REQ-SEC-113 | Audit; CTX-001 partial | Workspace skills and knowledge load only when the workspace is trusted. Injected items are annotated with their source. | safety | `skills.ts:31-33`, `knowledge.ts`, `agent.ts:3301` | TEST (SEC-05, AGT-10; test:cli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts::\"ACC-TRUST-04: workspace skills and knowledge load only when workspace is trusted, with source annotations\") | tested | ACC-TRUST-04 | RR | SEC-05, AGT-10 | SPEC-03 / PLAN-03 |\ndocs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:136:| REQ-SEC-114 | Audit; DEC-15 | No implicit standing rules (auto-extract is opt-in). No workspace writes in read-only mode. | safety | `knowledge.ts`, `agent.ts:4934` | TEST (AGT-10; test:cli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts::\"ACC-TRUST-05: no implicit standing rules (autoExtract opt-in) and read-only mode creates no .nexus/\") | tested | ACC-TRUST-05 | RR | AGT-10 | SPEC-03 / PLAN-03 |\ncli/test/doctor.test.ts:259:      // Untrusted: the workspace's own configuration is still inspected, and reported as not loaded.\ncli/test/doctor.test.ts:263:      assert.match(find(untrusted, \"mcp.config\")!.evidence.join(\"\\n\"), /\\.mcp\\.json: not loaded — this workspace is not trusted\\. Trust it with `nexus trust/);\ncli/test/doctor.test.ts:265:      assert.match(find(untrusted, \"plugins.config\")!.evidence.join(\"\\n\"), /p\\.mjs: not loaded — this workspace is not trusted/);\ncli/test/doctor.test.ts:266:      const { setWorkspaceTrusted } = await import(\"../src/workspace_trust.js\");\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/D_terminal.md:63:| TUI-07 | S2 | Default safety posture | sidecar.ts:935; repl.ts:69; quickstart.md:55 | SOURCE + DOC-CLAIM: default mode is `auto` (no approvals) for both surfaces; quickstart says \"Enter a small task, review requested actions\" | New users unknowingly grant the model unprompted write/shell authority | Decide v1 default (recommend review on first run / untrusted workspace); fix quickstart wording |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/D_terminal.md:147:| TUI-R6 | Default approval posture is an explicit, documented decision; first run in an untrusted workspace states the mode and how to change it | safety (TUI-07) | RELEASE-REQUIRED | PTY first-run screen contains mode + switch hint; docs match |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/F_requirements_history.md:38:| REQ-PRV-01..02 | DEST-001..002 | 4 trust boundaries (HOST_LOCAL, LOCAL_APPLIANCE, EXTERNAL_BOUNDED, EXTERNAL_PUBLIC); downward crossing = egress event | P2 §14 | DEMONSTRATED (egress.ts classifyDestination) | Y (WP05) | Maturity review 10-12 P0: workspace `.env` can select public endpoint; disclosure authorization not enforced (cites CLOUD_PROVIDERS_ASSESSMENT 10-11) |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/F_requirements_history.md:45:| REQ-SEC-43..45 | EXT-001..003 | Extensions in declared domains; presence ≠ execution authority; preconfigured trust without prompts | P2 §19 | SBIS | P | **Contradicted 10-12 T-03** (workspace plugins + `.mcp.json` executed with no trust, even `--read-only --approve`); remediated 3290234 (`nexus trust`) |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/F_requirements_history.md:66:| REQ-SEC-61 | B2/T-03 | Workspace plugins / `.mcp.json` only after `nexus trust`; never in `--read-only` | TOP_TIER B2; USER_MANUAL §4 | Implemented (workspace_trust.ts; plugins.ts:47-54,76; mcp.ts:509-511) | N |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/F_requirements_history.md:119:- **Trust boundaries in commitments:** operator config (`~/.config/nexus`) = credential authority; project `.env` may configure endpoints but not cloud destinations or credential origins; workspace extensions require `nexus trust`; home plugin dirs always trusted — including `~/.config/opencode/plugins` (undocumented, cli/src/plugins.ts:51).\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/F_requirements_history.md:142:| 1 | EXT-002 / B2 workspace plugins+MCP need trust | plugins.ts:47-54,76; mcp.ts:509-511; workspace_trust.ts present | HOLDS (regressed 10-12, fixed 3290234) |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/F_requirements_history.md:238:- **Public promises:** one-line installer pinned to v0.1.0-alpha.2 with SHA-256 verification, no root; Linux x86-64 glibc≥2.35 + Node≥20 only; OpenAI-compatible local endpoint first (LM Studio/vLLM), cloud opt-in; honest outcome labels (VERIFIED only from passing verifier); no default step ceiling; secrets tokenized on egress; workspace code needs trust; uninstall keeps user data. Maturity explicitly \"alpha, pre-1.0\".\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/A2_verification.md:37:- **Trust boundary.** Verifier commands run repository-defined code: Makefile recipes, package.json scripts, build.rs, conftest.py. They run through `bridge.executeTool(\"run_command\")` directly, so the dispatch approval gate (agent.ts:3947) never sees them. The sandbox applies only if `--sandbox`/read-only is set (tools.ts:755). No workspace-trust check applies.\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/A2_verification.md:52:| VER-01 | S2 | Any session on a cloned/untrusted repo, including `--approve` review mode | agent.ts:1602-1617 (baseline), 3432-3436 (oracle), 1630 (independence copy run); tester.ts:113-136 | SOURCE. Before the first file edit (agent.ts:4034, after the edit's own approval) and at every `task_completed`, Nexus runs `make test` / `npm test` / `cargo test` / `pytest` through `bridge.executeTool` directly. These run arbitrary repo-defined code with no approval prompt and no workspace-trust check (unlike MCP/plugins, workspace_trust.ts), and unsandboxed unless `--sandbox`. Review mode shows an approval for `edit_file` but never for the hidden repo script that follows. Builds on SEC-01; it is a separate path because it bypasses even review mode. | Cloning a hostile repo and asking for any edit runs its test script on the host without a prompt. | RELEASE-REQUIRED: gate discovered verifiers on workspace trust or a one-time approval of the exact command (shown with its script body), and run them sandboxed when one is available. Document this. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/A2_verification.md:134:| R-VER-1 | Repo-defined verifier commands (discovered) run only in a trusted workspace or after one approval that shows the command and script body. They run sandboxed when a sandbox is available. | safety | RELEASE-REQUIRED | An untrusted temp repo with `\"test\":\"touch /tmp/pwn\"` plus an edit request: no file created without a prompt, in both default and --approve modes. |\ncli/src/plugins.ts:7:import { isWorkspaceTrusted, WORKSPACE_PLUGIN_DIRS } from \"./workspace_trust.js\";\ncli/src/plugins.ts:65:  constructor(workspace: string, options?: { readOnly?: boolean; trusted?: boolean }) {\ncli/src/plugins.ts:68:    this.trusted = options?.trusted !== undefined ? options.trusted : isWorkspaceTrusted(this.workspace, this.readOnly);\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:14:| cli/src/workspace_trust.ts | full read |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:20:| cli/src/session_export.ts, file_diff.ts, syntax.ts, diagnostics.ts, core/resources.ts | grep only: no exec/fs-write/permission calls found; not reviewed further |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:35:  - Workspace MCP configs and plugin JS are gated by `~/.config/nexus/trusted_workspaces.json` (workspace_trust.ts:35).\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:37:  - `.nexus-security.json` is read from the untrusted workspace (security.ts:48). It can only add entries, except that `allowedDomains` narrows egress and `allowExternalSymlinks` is never consumed.\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:52:| SEC-04 | S2 | read-only mode | authority.ts:44-136; tools.ts:485-495, 755-779 | RUNTIME (Repro R2). `isSafeReadOnlyCommand` allows `cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa`, `cat /etc/passwd`, `tail -f /dev/null`. The allowlist does not confine paths. The sandbox runs `--ro-bind / /` with the network unshared, so host secrets are readable and returned to the (possibly cloud) model. | `--read-only` reads like \"safe to point at an untrusted repo\", but it still discloses home-directory secrets. | RELEASE-REQUIRED: in read-only/sandbox mode, mask `$HOME` (tmpfs over home, then ro-bind the workspace only), or path-confine the allowlisted commands. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:53:| SEC-05 | S2 | untrusted repo opened | skills.ts:31-33; agent.ts:3301,3789 | SOURCE. Workspace skills (SKILL.md) are loaded and advertised in the system prompt with no workspace-trust check, unlike MCP and plugins (workspace_trust.ts:5-24). `activate_skill` is non-mutating, so it is never approved. | Persistent prompt injection from a cloned repo. Combined with SEC-01 this gives command execution. | RELEASE-REQUIRED: gate workspace skill directories on workspace trust, or show them as untrusted and require approval to activate. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:115:- No shell is used for git (git2) or for worktree ops (execFileSync argv). MCP children get an env allowlist (mcp.ts:56-77). Workspace MCP and plugins require explicit trust, and never get it in read-only mode (workspace_trust.ts:35-53, mcp.ts:509-531).\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:116:- Session state: schema version with fail-closed on future versions, legacy migration, identity and workspace binding on load, redaction before persist, and a fsync'd append-only compaction archive with a commit protocol (state.ts:338-460; durable_file.ts). The trust store is written atomically with 0600/0700 (workspace_trust.ts:71-76).\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:136:| R-SEC-5 | Workspace skills are subject to workspace trust. | safety | RELEASE-REQUIRED | An untrusted fixture repo with `skills/x/SKILL.md` does not appear in the system prompt or `/skills` without trust. |\ncli/src/core/workspace_path.ts:34:    if (!workspacePathCapabilities.has(this)) throw new Error(\"Workspace identity lacks trusted runtime provenance\");\ncli/src/doctor.ts:26:import { untrustedWorkspaceExtensions, WORKSPACE_MCP_FILES } from \"./workspace_trust.js\";\ncli/src/doctor.ts:660:  // Every configuration file is inspected, including a workspace's own that is not loaded because it is untrusted.\ncli/src/doctor.ts:692:  for (const file of untrustedWorkspaceExtensions(workspace).filter((f) => WORKSPACE_MCP_FILES.includes(path.basename(f)))) {\ncli/src/doctor.ts:693:    problems.push(`${file}: not loaded — this workspace is not trusted. Trust it with \\`nexus trust ${workspace}\\` to use its MCP servers.`);\ncli/src/doctor.ts:705:  for (const file of untrustedWorkspaceExtensions(workspace).filter((f) => !WORKSPACE_MCP_FILES.includes(path.basename(f)))) {\ncli/src/doctor.ts:706:    risky.push(`${file}: not loaded — this workspace is not trusted. Trust it with \\`nexus trust ${workspace}\\` to load its plugins.`);\ncli/src/doctor.ts:723:  if (risky.length) return f(\"plugins.config\", \"extensions\", \"warn\", `${risky.length} plugin problem(s): untrusted workspace plugins are not loaded; world-writable plugin files could be changed by anyone. ${note}`, [...risky, ...found.slice(0, 10)], \"Trust the workspace (`nexus trust`) or remove its plugins; restrict writable files with `chmod o-w <file>`.\");\ncli/src/workspace_trust.ts:11: * is not trusted (or the session is read-only). Empty when there are none or the workspace is trusted.\ncli/src/workspace_trust.ts:13:export function untrustedWorkspaceExtensions(workspace: string, readOnly = false): string[] {\ncli/src/workspace_trust.ts:28:  return path.join(os.homedir(), \".config\", \"nexus\", \"trusted_workspaces.json\");\ncli/src/workspace_trust.ts:32: * Check if the given workspace is explicitly trusted by the operator.\ncli/src/workspace_trust.ts:33: * In --read-only mode, workspace trust never authorizes executing project plugins/MCP.\ncli/src/workspace_trust.ts:55:/** The trusted workspaces (real paths), as recorded in the trust store. */\ncli/src/workspace_trust.ts:66:/** Records (or removes) the operator's trust in a workspace. Returns its real path; throws when the store cannot be written. */\ncli/src/workspace_trust.ts:67:export function setWorkspaceTrusted(workspace: string, trusted: boolean): string {\ndocs/cloudflare/CREATE_FAILURE_DELTA_INVESTIGATION_2026-10-02.md:34:The failed run recorded one CREATE invocation, no returned token ID or credential, no Git proof, and no revoke. Its first classification (`DEFINITE_PROVIDER_REJECTION`, `permissionDenied=true`) is not supported by retained provider-origin evidence. Independent review found that broad permission-related text can also come from local CLI failures. The authoritative interpretation is therefore:\ncli/src/mcp.ts:7:import { isWorkspaceTrusted, WORKSPACE_MCP_FILES } from \"./workspace_trust.js\";\ncli/src/mcp.ts:509:export function mcpConfigFiles(workspace: string, trusted: boolean = false, readOnly: boolean = false): string[] {\ncli/src/mcp.ts:511:  if (trusted && !readOnly) configs.push(...WORKSPACE_MCP_FILES.map((file) => path.join(workspace, file)));\ncli/src/mcp.ts:528:  constructor(workspace: string, options: { readOnly?: boolean; trusted?: boolean } = {}) {\ncli/src/mcp.ts:531:    this.trusted = options.trusted ?? isWorkspaceTrusted(this.workspace, this.readOnly);\ncli/src/mcp.ts:545:    for (const file of mcpConfigFiles(this.workspace, this.trusted, this.readOnly)) {\ncli/src/agent.ts:146:import { untrustedWorkspaceExtensions } from \"./workspace_trust.js\";\ncli/src/agent.ts:1345:      const skipped = untrustedWorkspaceExtensions(this.config.workspace, this.config.readOnly);\ncli/src/agent.ts:1350:          : `Workspace MCP servers and plugins are not loaded because this workspace is not trusted (${names}). Run \\`nexus trust\\` in it to allow them.`;\ndocs/v1.0.0/AUDIT.md:37:  - workspace trust;\ndocs/v1.0.0/AUDIT.md:138:| Tools, security, persistence: `tools, safe_fs, sandbox, security, session, session_export, checkpoint, flight, evidence, git, worktree, mcp, plugins, skills, browser, lsp, file_diff, syntax, diagnostics, workspace_trust`, `core/{authority, resources, durable_file, persistence_protection, capabilities, workspace_path, independent_check, tool_groups}` | 28 | Full read for security-critical files. Grep or skim for session_export, file_diff, syntax, diagnostics, resources, browser, lsp, mcp transports. | C_tools_security_persistence; this file (workspace_trust, workspace_path, tool_groups, independent_check, approval_review, read by lead) | MCP remote transports, browser.ts and lsp.ts got only a skim review: **qualification gap** |\ncli/src/index.ts:386:    const { untrustedWorkspaceExtensions } = await import(\"./workspace_trust.js\");\ncli/src/index.ts:388:      console.log(pc.yellow(`Not loaded (workspace not trusted; run \\`nexus trust\\`): ${file}`));\ncli/src/index.ts:425:    const { untrustedWorkspaceExtensions } = await import(\"./workspace_trust.js\");\ncli/src/index.ts:427:      console.log(pc.yellow(`Not loaded (workspace not trusted; run \\`nexus trust\\`): ${file}`));\ncli/src/index.ts:444:  .option(\"--remove\", \"Stop trusting the workspace\")\ncli/src/index.ts:445:  .option(\"--list\", \"List trusted workspaces\")\ncli/src/index.ts:447:    const { listTrustedWorkspaces, setWorkspaceTrusted } = await import(\"./workspace_trust.js\");\ncli/src/index.ts:450:      console.log(list.length ? list.join(\"\\n\") : \"No trusted workspaces.\");\ncli/src/index.ts:460:    console.log(options.remove ? `No longer trusted: ${real}` : `Trusted: ${real}\\nIts .mcp.json servers and plugins will be loaded (they run code from this workspace). Undo with \\`nexus trust --remove\\`.`);\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_APPLICATION_MATURITY_REVIEW_2026-10-12.md:19:**Maturity gap:** Configuration selection and authorization are not the same thing. Make destination trust explicit, visible before first transmission, policy-controlled per workspace/task, and auditable. Include bulk context and auxiliary requests (embeddings, compaction, delegated agents), and cost ceilings for billable destinations. Treat the provider assessment as a dated snapshot and revalidate source before implementation.\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_APPLICATION_MATURITY_REVIEW_2026-10-12.md:21:**Acceptance evidence:** negative tests proving untrusted workspace configuration cannot silently redirect context; positive tests for explicit operator approval; end-to-end audit/receipt naming destination/provider/model and data class; enforceable spend/request limits.\ncli/src/security.ts:27:  // The operator's list of trusted workspaces: the agent must not trust a workspace for itself.\ncli/src/security.ts:28:  \"trusted_workspaces.json\",\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/A_core_agent.md:53:| AGT-10 | S3 | Knowledge auto-extraction | knowledge.ts:34-43, 206-231; agent.ts:4934-4936, 3302 | SOURCE. After every depth-0 turn `autoExtract(promptText)` stores prompt fragments matching `always|never|do not|must …` / `use|run|prefer npm|cargo…` as permanent \"rule\"/\"idiom\" entries in `<workspace>/.nexus/knowledge.json`, which `buildSystemPrompt` injects into every future session. Constructor `mkdir`s `<workspace>/.nexus` even in `--read-only`. A workspace-supplied `knowledge.json` is injected without the workspace-trust gate applied to MCP/plugins. | Silent standing instructions from one-off prompts (\"never run the tests\" in a task) or from a cloned repo; read-only mode writes into the workspace. | Make opt-in; no writes in read-only; trust-gate injection. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/A_core_agent.md:112:| AGT-R8 | No implicit standing rules from prompts; no workspace writes in read-only mode; workspace-supplied knowledge gated by workspace trust. | safety (AGT-10) | RELEASE-REQUIRED | Read-only run leaves no `.nexus/`; prompt \"never run tests\" does not appear in next session's system prompt. |\ndocs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/trust_inference.md:53:### T-03 Opening a workspace executes workspace-supplied code (plugins and MCP servers) with no trust prompt, even under `--read-only` and `--approve`\ndocs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/trust_inference.md:58:- Expected: workspace-provided executables are not run until the operator trusts that workspace; read-only mode never executes them.\ndocs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/trust_inference.md:68:- Remediation: load workspace-scoped plugins/MCP only after an explicit, persisted per-workspace trust decision (hash of the config/plugin\n.git/nexus-claims/FILE_cli_test_git_exclude.test.ts/owner.json:15:  \"review_note\": \"plan05_readonly_review ACCEPT exact head a568a0da3c032576e7dbb96f02cbfddbcd61bcc3; verifies .nexus ignore behavior, higher-priority/repository negations, duplicate rules, nested workspaces and filesystem boundaries.\",\n.git/nexus-claims/PLAN-07_INSTALLER_ATTESTATION_FIX_20261025/owner.json:7:  \"scope\": \"fail-closed trusted-gh attestation enforcement in install.sh and installer smoke fixtures\",\n.git/nexus-claims/FILE_cli_src_sandbox.ts/owner.json:7:  \"scope\": \"read-only Bubblewrap synthetic root, descriptor-pinned workspace, and Docker fail-closed behavior\",\n.git/nexus-claims/FILE_cli_src_worktree.ts/owner.json:8:  \"scope\": \"PR200 review correction: defer .nexus Git exclusion mutation from WorktreeManager construction to actual worktree creation; regression tests for non-mutating startup and fail-closed first write\",\n.git/nexus-claims/FILE_cli_test_flight_exclude.test.ts/owner.json:8:  \"scope\": \"PR200 review correction: defer .nexus Git exclusion mutation from WorktreeManager construction to actual worktree creation; regression tests for non-mutating startup and fail-closed first write\",\n.git/nexus-claims/FILE_cli_test_readonly_home.test.ts/owner.json:7:  \"scope\": \"read-only workspace identity replacement, sandbox cancellation and timeout regression; final exact-branch targeted validation\",\n.git/nexus-claims/FILE_experiments_cloudflare-artifacts-vertical-slice_trusted-broker-bundle_package-lock.json/owner.json:8:  \"scope\": \"Trusted broker bundle package-lock.json only for security patch updates.\",\n.git/nexus-claims/FILE_experiments_cloudflare-artifacts-vertical-slice_trusted-broker-bundle_package.json/owner.json:8:  \"scope\": \"Trusted broker bundle package.json only for patched dependency overrides.\",\n# Running multiple agents on this plan without collisions\n\nThese rules apply to every agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Nexus itself, or a human) that implements any part of this package at the same time as another. The repository's root `AGENTS.md` points here. The rules come from incidents recorded in `tasks/lessons.md`:\n\n- a concurrent agent overwrote newer evidence;\n- full test suites were contaminated by edits made mid-run;\n- `pkill -f` killed the agent's own shell;\n- HEAD moved during a pause.\n\nThey also follow from audit finding PER-03: the test suite writes into the real `~/.nexus`.\n\n## 1. Unit of work: one claimed PR slice per agent\n\n- The unit of concurrent work is **one PR slice** from a plan, for example `PLAN-01/PR-1a`. Every slice lists its tasks and its safe change boundary in its plan.\n- An agent works on exactly one claimed slice at a time.\n- An agent must not edit files outside its slice's boundary. The exceptions are the ledger rows and the shared files covered by §4. If the work needs a file outside the boundary, the agent stops and records a **boundary request** (§6). It does not edit the file.\n\n## 2. Claiming a slice (atomic and machine-local)\n\nClaims live in the repository's shared git directory. They are visible to every worktree, and they are never committed.\n\n```sh\nCLAIMS=\"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\"\nmkdir -p \"$CLAIMS\"\nSLICE=\"PLAN-01_PR-1a\"\nif mkdir \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\" 2>/dev/null; then          # mkdir is atomic: exactly one agent wins\n  printf '{\"agent\":\"%s\",\"branch\":\"%s\",\"since\":\"%s\",\"pid\":%s}\\n' \\\n    \"$AGENT_NAME\" \"v1/plan-01-pr-1a\" \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ)\" \"$$\" > \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\"\nelse\n  echo \"claimed by: $(cat \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\")\"; exit 1\nfi\n```\n\n- **Release** the claim by deleting its directory (`rm -r \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\"`) after the slice merges or is abandoned. Write one line about the outcome to `$CLAIMS/../nexus-claims.log`.\n- **Hot-file claims** use the same mechanism with the name `FILE_<path with / replaced by _>`, for example `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts`. See §4.\n- **Stale claims.** A claim older than 24 h whose PID is gone may be broken, but only by the owner, or by an agent that first records the broken claim in the log.\n- If the MCP Agent Mail server is available, its file reservations (`file_reservation_paths`) may be used as well. The git-common-dir claim stays authoritative, because every agent can see it without extra tooling.\n\n## 3. Isolation: worktree, branch, HOME, ports, processes\n\n| Resource | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Source | One `git worktree` per claimed slice: `git worktree add ../nexus-wt/<slice> -b v1/<plan>-<slice> origin/main`. Never edit `~/nexus-code` itself. It is the owner's checkout, and `/usr/local/bin/nexus` runs from it. |\n| Branch | `v1/plan-NN-pr-Nx`. One branch per slice. Never commit to `main` directly. |\n| HOME and state | Until PLAN-08 PR-8a lands, every test, build and probe run sets `HOME=$(mktemp -d)` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=\"$HOME/.config\"`. After PR-8a, the test setup enforces this, but scripts and probes outside `npm test` must still set it. **Never** run anything that writes to the real `~/.nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. |\n| Test runs | Full suites run on a **snapshot copy** of the worktree (`git worktree add` at the commit under test, or `git archive`), never on the tree being edited. `tsx` reads `src/` live. |\n| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n| Ports | Mock servers bind `127.0.0.1:0` (an ephemeral port) and publish the chosen port to a file. Fixed ports are forbidden. |\n| Processes | Kill only PIDs you started, taken from `$!` or `ps -eo pid,args \\| grep -v grep`. **Never `pkill -f` or `killall`.** Long background jobs are stopped through the agent's own task controls. |\n| Inference | There is one inference host (DEC-16). Any run that uses the gateway takes the claim `GATEWAY` first. That includes eval runs, live checks and stripped-proxy runs. One gateway run at a time, which keeps eval timings valid and prevents overload. Small cloud checks (E5) take `CLOUD` to avoid duplicate spend. |\n| Credentials | Agents never read or copy `~/.config/nexus/credentials.json`, `config.env` values, or gateway tokens. The stripped proxy reads the token file itself. |\n\n## 4. Hot files: serialise, do not merge blindly\n\nThese files are touched by several plans. An agent editing one must hold its `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit, test, push, merge, then release.\n\n| File | Plans that touch it | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cli/src/agent.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 11 | Claim required. Keep each change local to the line ranges the plan names. Rebase immediately before merge and re-run that slice's tests on the rebased snapshot. |\n| `cli/src/index.ts` | 01, 03, 04, 06, 08, 10, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-06 PR-6b (unified launcher) lands first.** Other plans add commands and flags afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tools.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 06, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-02 PR-2b (signal plumbing) lands before PLAN-03 PR-3c (`safeFetch`)**. |\n| `cli/src/sidecar.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/doctor.ts` | 01, 03, 05, 06, 08 | Claim required. Each plan adds **new check functions** in separate blocks. Only PLAN-06 PR-6d changes existing checks. |\n| `cli/src/client.ts` | 03, 05, 08, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-03 PR-3a (env layering) lands before any PLAN-05 slice.** |\n| `cli/src/repl.ts` | 01, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/config.ts`, `security.ts`, `sandbox.ts`, `mcp.ts`, `plugins.ts` | 01, 03 | Claim required. PLAN-01 PR-1e and PLAN-03 must not run at the same time on `sandbox.ts` or `security.ts`. |\n| `cli/src/core/capabilities.ts` | 01, 02 | **PLAN-01 PR-1a (effect classes) lands first.** PLAN-02 PR-2c adds schema fields afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tester.ts` | 01, 11 | **PLAN-01 PR-1c lands before PLAN-11 PR-11b/11c.** |\n| `cli/src/core/state.ts` | 04, 08 | **PLAN-08 PR-8a (HOME isolation) lands before everything.** |\n| `core/src/runner.rs`, `core/src/main.rs` | 01, 02, 06 | Claim required. The protocol version number is owned by PLAN-02 PR-2a. PLAN-06 PR-6c only reads it. |\n| `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/{live_state,live_view}.rs` | 01, 02 | Claim required |\n| `package.json`, `cli/package.json`, `core/Cargo.toml`, lockfiles | 01, 06, 07, 08 | **Only one agent at a time.** Version fields are written only by `scripts/sync-version.mjs`. Dependency changes are separate commits that touch only the lockfile and manifest. |\n| `harness.sh`, `install.sh`, `scripts/package-linux.sh`, `.github/workflows/*` | 05, 06, 07, 09, C1 | Claim required. Workflow edits are separate commits. |\n| `cli/dist/**` | all, while tracked | Do not commit `cli/dist` from slices. PLAN-07 PR-7d untracks it (DEC-10). Until that merges, only the merging agent rebuilds and commits dist, in a final separate commit, after rebasing. |\n| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `quickstart.md` | 05, 09 and doc tasks in every plan | Edit only the sections your slice changes. Hold a short `FILE_` claim. |\n\n### Ledger updates (`docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md`)\n\nA slice changes only the rows for its own requirement IDs: the status, test-reference and evidence cells, in place. It never reorders, adds or removes rows, and never edits another slice's rows. New requirements discovered during work go to §6, not into the ledger. Because rows are independent lines, git merges these edits cleanly. If a conflict appears anyway, resolve it row by row, keeping both changes.\n\n## 5. Commit and merge discipline\n\n1. Before **every** commit, compare `git rev-parse HEAD` with the commit you last made. Then run `git log <yours>..HEAD` on the branch. If anything foreign appears, stop and reconcile. Never commit generated artifacts (evidence, dist, lockfiles) over a branch that moved (lessons 2026-10-13).\n2. Commits are scoped to the slice. Do not reformat or clean up files outside the boundary.\n3. Merge order follows the precedence rules in §4 and the ROADMAP milestone order. Within a milestone, the slice with fewer hot-file claims merges first.\n4. Rebase onto `origin/main` immediately before merging. Re-run the slice's targeted tests, plus `make test`, on a snapshot of the rebased commit. Merge only if both pass.\n5. Pushing, tagging, publishing and changing GitHub state (issues, milestones, releases) are **owner-approved actions** (DECISIONS X-list). Agents prepare them and do not perform them.\n6. Preserve unrelated user work: the untracked `nexus-continuity-validation/`, the stashes, and any dirty files you did not create. Never stash, reset, clean or check out over them.\n\n## 6. Coordination records\n\n| Record | Where | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Claims | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` | Who owns which slice or file right now |\n| Claim log | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims.log` (append-only, one line per event) | Claim, release, broken claim, boundary request |\n| Boundary requests and discovered requirements | The PR description, under the heading `Needs from other slices`. Mirrored as one line in the claim log. | So the owning slice's agent can pick it up. Never fixed silently out of scope. |\n| Slice evidence | `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/<plan>-<slice>/` (new directory per slice) | No shared evidence files, so agents cannot overwrite each other's evidence |\n\n## 7. Safe parallel lanes\n\nThe following groupings can run at the same time with no hot-file contention beyond §4 claims. They are listed by milestone.\n\n| Phase | Parallel lanes |\n|---|---|\n| M0 | (a) PLAN-08 PR-8a, which must merge first. Then in parallel: (b) PLAN-06 PR-6a, (c) PLAN-09 PR-9a. |\n| M1 | (a) PLAN-01 PR-1a → PR-1b → PR-1c (sequential, owns `capabilities.ts`, `agent.ts` dispatch). (b) PLAN-02 PR-2a (Rust only). (c) PLAN-03 PR-3a → PR-3b (config/egress). (d) PLAN-11 PR-11a (`parser.ts`) and PR-11b (`tester.ts`, after PR-1c). Lanes (b), (c) and (d) do not touch `capabilities.ts`. |\n| M2 (alongside M1) | (a) PLAN-07 PR-7a/7b/7c (workflows, scripts). (b) PLAN-06 PR-6b after PLAN-01 PR-1b (shares `index.ts` flags). |\n| M3 | (a) PLAN-04 (`agent.ts` turn, operation runner). (b) PLAN-05 (`client.ts`, provider modules). (c) PLAN-10 harness (`eval/`, no product files). (d) Held-out task authoring by the agent author, **outside the repository**: no access to Nexus source or the development set (DEC-05). |\n| M4 (alongside M3) | PLAN-08 PR-8b..8e. Coordinate with PLAN-04 on `state.ts` through `FILE_` claims. |\n| M5 | **Serial on `GATEWAY`.** The eval runner, the stripped-proxy runs and the independent reviewer's live task each take the `GATEWAY` claim. The terminal matrix (E3) and the install matrix (E2) can run in parallel with gateway runs. |\n\n## 8. Independence constraints\n\n- The held-out task author must not see Nexus source, the development tasks or tuning history. It runs in a separate session with only the task schema and external repositories. Its output is sealed by a sha256 manifest committed before RC1.\n- The independent reviewer in ACCEPTANCE §8 must not be the agent or session that implemented the slices under review.\n- An implementing agent never edits `eval/heldout-manifest.json`, scorer thresholds or ACCEPTANCE §6.4 after the freeze.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":39260}},"started_at_ms":1792905670032,"completed_at_ms":1792905670033}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T05:21:11.269Z","ordinal":325452,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a17181-7265-7813-af06-11129d76154c","call_id":"call_68fbe6197dd54556bf2651588504b32e","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.4 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 11554)\nTotal output lines: 244\n\ndocs/CLIENT_GUIDE.md:26:The client executes workspace tools and shell commands with the current user's permissions. `--approve` / review mode prompts for edits and shell execution. Automatic approval is convenient but gives the configured model authority to request those actions; review consequential changes and command output yourself. `--read-only` refuses mutating tools and commands. `--sandbox` can use a supported bubblewrap or Docker runtime; availability and guarantees vary by host, so do not treat the option name as proof of isolation. A running shell command cannot always be force-stopped after it has started.\ndocs/NEXUS-CLOUD-PROVIDERS-PLAN.md:55:3. Configuration precedence and project trust: flags, environment, user configuration, workspace configuration, restored sessions. Identify what can currently change a destination or credential source.\ndocs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:497:| **EXT-002** | The discovery or presence of executable extension code within a workspace (e.g., in `.nexus/plugins` or `workspace/plugins`) MUST NOT confer host execution authority. Workspace extensions MUST NOT be executed without authority established by configured policy. | Opening or inspecting an untrusted third-party repository containing JavaScript plugins MUST NOT execute that code automatically. |\ndocs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:776:| **F-04** | Automatic execution of untrusted workspace plugins (Demonstrated) | **EXT-002, EXT-003** | Fully resolved incorporating Operator Decision 2 (policy-based execution authority). |\ndocs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:824:   - *Reconciled Change:* Strengthened by `EXT-002` and `EXT-003` to mandate that discovery/presence in a workspace does not confer execution authority. Execution requires configured policy authority, while preconfigured trust eliminates mandatory interactive prompts, resolving `F-04` and Operator Decision 2.\ndocs/PHASE1_SYSTEM_RECONSTRUCTION.md:88:| Validation | TestRunResult, verifiedWorktreeCompletion boolean, SynthesizerState, tool output, evaluator result. | Discovery success advisory; configured verifier alone authorizes auto merge. TDD/evaluator/manual test have separate result semantics. | No durable validation artifact bound to exact mutation set/workspace/commit. | #11 tests prove configured success/failure behavior, not semantic quality of trusted command. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/README.md:26:| 03 | Untrusted workspace inputs and egress boundary | [SPEC-03](specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | [PLAN-03](plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | M1 | SEC-110, 111, 115, 116 |\ndocs/USER_MANUAL.md:295:- **Workspace Trust**: A workspace's own `.mcp.json` / `mcp.json` servers and `.nexus/plugins/` / `plugins/` modules run code from the workspace, so they are loaded only after you trust it: `nexus trust [path]` (undo with `nexus trust --remove`, list with `nexus trust --list`; recorded in `~/.config/nexus/trusted_workspaces.json`, which the agent's file tools cannot write). Untrusted, they are not loaded and Nexus says so (a notice at start, `nexus doctor`, `nexus mcp`, `nexus plugins`); in `--read-only` mode they are never loaded. Servers and plugins in your home configuration (`~/.config/nexus/`, `~/.nexus/`) are always loaded.\ndocs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:9:| [DEC-01](#dec-01-default-execution-posture) | Default execution posture | Review by default; auto only in trusted workspaces | No (resolved) |\ndocs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:36:| **C (chosen)** | Posture follows workspace trust. **Untrusted workspace → review** for `write_workspace`, `exec`, `egress` and `context_inject`. **Trusted workspace → auto** for `write_workspace` and `exec`; `egress` asks once per origin per session. `--yes` or `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto` force auto. `--sandbox` stays available. | Safe for cloned or unknown repos. Low friction on the operator's own repos after one `nexus trust .`. Reuses the existing trust store, so there is no new concept. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:39:**Rationale.** The threat that matters is content from an untrusted repository or the web. Trust is the existing per-workspace boundary that MCP and plugins already honour. Option C closes SEC-01 and VER-01 on exactly the inputs that cause them, and keeps the daily-driver experience on trusted repos.\ndocs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:172:**Chosen:** off by default; opt-in with `NEXUS_KNOWLEDGE_AUTOEXTRACT=1`, with a visible notice each time a rule is saved. Workspace knowledge is loaded only when the workspace is trusted.\ncli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:11:import { isWorkspaceTrusted, listTrustedWorkspaces, setWorkspaceTrusted, untrustedWorkspaceExtensions } from \"../src/workspace_trust.js\";\ncli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:33:describe(\"workspace trust\", () => {\ncli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:34:  test(\"workspace plugins load only when the workspace is trusted, and never in read-only mode\", async () => {\ncli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:68:      const store = path.join(home, \".config\", \"nexus\", \"trusted_workspaces.json\");\ndocs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:96:| EXT-002 | 15: MCP, Plugin & Extension | The discovery or presence of executable extension code within a workspace (e.g., in `.nexus/plugins` or `workspace/plugins`) MUST NOT confer host execution authority. Workspace extensions MUST NOT be executed without authority established by configured policy. | Opening or inspecting an untrusted third-party repository containing JavaScript plugins MUST NOT execute that code automatically. | Phase 2 §19 | `cli/src/plugins.ts, cli/src/mcp.ts`; cli/test/harness.test.ts | SATISFIED_BY_INTEGRATED_SYSTEM | None | Satisfied |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:5:| Status | Ready. Default posture resolved by [DEC-01](../DECISIONS.md#dec-01-default-execution-posture) (option C: review in untrusted workspaces, auto in trusted ones). |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:33:**Verifier runs bypass the gate entirely (VER-01).** Before the first edit (change baseline, `agent.ts:1602-1617`) and at every `task_completed` (`agent.ts:3432-3436`), Nexus runs the repository's own test command (`make test` / `npm test` / `cargo test` / `pytest`, chosen by `tester.ts:113-136`) through `bridge.executeTool` directly. These calls skip `dispatchToolCall`, so even review mode never asks, and no workspace-trust check applies. Opening a hostile repo and approving one `edit_file` therefore runs that repo's arbitrary test script on the host. `/test` and `nexus eval` run `execSync` on the host, bypassing the sandbox and blocking the event loop (VER-14).\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:58:5. **REQ-TUI-102.** At session start, both surfaces state the active posture, how to change it, and whether the workspace is trusted.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:62:   - In an untrusted workspace, the first verifier run requires a one-time approval. The approval shows the exact command and the script body it resolves to (for example the `scripts.test` entry, or the Makefile `test:` recipe).\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:63:   - In a trusted workspace, they run without asking under the auto posture.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:96:| `exec` | run_command, run_tests, LSP server spawn, plugin and MCP tool calls | refused, except the confined read-only allowlist | ask | allowed if the workspace is trusted; otherwise ask (DEC-01 option C) |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:133:Line 1 of the TUI header and the REPL banner: `mode: review · sandbox: off · workspace: untrusted (nexus trust .)`. `nexus doctor` reports the same three facts.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:140:- Persisted sessions do **not** persist an approval mode. Resume uses the current launch posture. This avoids resurrecting authority, per PERS-004.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:144:- Changing the default for untrusted workspaces from `auto` to review (DEC-01 option C) is a user-visible change. Release notes and the startup banner must state it. Provide `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto` and `nexus trust .` + `--yes` for scripted users.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:168:| ACC-SAFE-01 | Fresh HOME, `nexus` (TUI) and `nexus repl`, mock gateway scripted to call `run_command \"echo x > ../outside\"`: in an untrusted workspace the call is not executed without an approval panel. After `nexus trust .` it runs without a panel (DEC-01 option C). | PTY |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:181:- **DEC-01** is resolved as option C (review in untrusted workspaces, auto in trusted ones). Nothing in this spec is blocked.\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:42:| 5 | Route verifier executions through the policy as `exec`. When not trusted and no prior approval exists, emit the approval with `kind: \"verifier\"`, the command, and the resolved script body (read `package.json` `scripts.test`, the Makefile `test:` recipe, `Cargo.toml`/`pytest.ini` presence). Cache the approval per (workspace, command, script digest) for the session. | `agent.ts:1602-1630,3432-3436,4034`; `tester.ts:113-136` (add `describeVerifier()`); `approval_review.ts` (new kind) | 3 |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:1:# SPEC-03: Untrusted workspace inputs and the egress boundary\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:14:Nexus's threat model, as its docs state it, is that the operator's own configuration (`~/.config/nexus`) holds authority. An opened workspace is data until the operator trusts it (USER_MANUAL §4; `cli/src/workspace_trust.ts`). Workspace MCP configs and plugins honour this: they need `nexus trust` and never run in `--read-only`. Several other inputs that come from the workspace or the network do not:\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:42:4. **REQ-SEC-113.** Workspace skills and workspace `knowledge.json` are loaded only for trusted workspaces. In an untrusted workspace they are listed by `/skills` as \"untrusted (not loaded)\", and `activate_skill` on one is refused with the `nexus trust` hint.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:46:6. **REQ-SEC-115.** Nexus loads plugins only from its own documented directories (`~/.config/nexus/plugins`, `~/.nexus/plugins`, and trusted workspace dirs). `~/.config/opencode/plugins` is removed.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:87:In `skills.ts:31-33`, split skill roots into home roots (always) and workspace roots (only if `isWorkspaceTrusted(ws, readOnly)`). In `knowledge.ts`, apply the same gating for loading, make the constructor lazy (no `mkdir`), and add an opt-in flag for `autoExtract`. The model-facing system prompt marks injected skill and knowledge content with its source path and trust state. This is a partial answer to CTX-001 (provenance per injected item) and is recorded in DEC-04.\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:1:# PLAN-03: Untrusted workspace inputs and the egress boundary\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:33:| 6 | Trust-gate workspace skill roots and workspace `knowledge.json`. `/skills` lists untrusted entries as such. `activate_skill` on an untrusted skill is refused with a hint. Injected blocks are annotated with source and trust state (partial CTX-001). | `skills.ts:31-33`; `knowledge.ts`; `agent.ts:3301,3789`; `slash/extension_commands.ts` | — |\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:56:- **`workspace_trust.test.ts`**: extend for skills and knowledge (ACC-TRUST-04/05).\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:86:| Q4 | Enforces trustworthy execution boundaries | every mutating/egress capability gated by mode and context; untrusted workspaces cannot execute code; secrets never leave or persist in plaintext |\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:101:| Q4 boundar…5554 tokens truncated…us-claims/FILE_cli_test_readonly_home.test.ts/owner.json:7:  \"scope\": \"read-only workspace identity replacement, sandbox cancellation and timeout regression; final exact-branch targeted validation\",\n.git/nexus-claims/FILE_experiments_cloudflare-artifacts-vertical-slice_trusted-broker-bundle_package-lock.json/owner.json:8:  \"scope\": \"Trusted broker bundle package-lock.json only for security patch updates.\",\n.git/nexus-claims/FILE_experiments_cloudflare-artifacts-vertical-slice_trusted-broker-bundle_package.json/owner.json:8:  \"scope\": \"Trusted broker bundle package.json only for patched dependency overrides.\",\n# Running multiple agents on this plan without collisions\n\nThese rules apply to every agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Nexus itself, or a human) that implements any part of this package at the same time as another. The repository's root `AGENTS.md` points here. The rules come from incidents recorded in `tasks/lessons.md`:\n\n- a concurrent agent overwrote newer evidence;\n- full test suites were contaminated by edits made mid-run;\n- `pkill -f` killed the agent's own shell;\n- HEAD moved during a pause.\n\nThey also follow from audit finding PER-03: the test suite writes into the real `~/.nexus`.\n\n## 1. Unit of work: one claimed PR slice per agent\n\n- The unit of concurrent work is **one PR slice** from a plan, for example `PLAN-01/PR-1a`. Every slice lists its tasks and its safe change boundary in its plan.\n- An agent works on exactly one claimed slice at a time.\n- An agent must not edit files outside its slice's boundary. The exceptions are the ledger rows and the shared files covered by §4. If the work needs a file outside the boundary, the agent stops and records a **boundary request** (§6). It does not edit the file.\n\n## 2. Claiming a slice (atomic and machine-local)\n\nClaims live in the repository's shared git directory. They are visible to every worktree, and they are never committed.\n\n```sh\nCLAIMS=\"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\"\nmkdir -p \"$CLAIMS\"\nSLICE=\"PLAN-01_PR-1a\"\nif mkdir \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\" 2>/dev/null; then          # mkdir is atomic: exactly one agent wins\n  printf '{\"agent\":\"%s\",\"branch\":\"%s\",\"since\":\"%s\",\"pid\":%s}\\n' \\\n    \"$AGENT_NAME\" \"v1/plan-01-pr-1a\" \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ)\" \"$$\" > \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\"\nelse\n  echo \"claimed by: $(cat \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\")\"; exit 1\nfi\n```\n\n- **Release** the claim by deleting its directory (`rm -r \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\"`) after the slice merges or is abandoned. Write one line about the outcome to `$CLAIMS/../nexus-claims.log`.\n- **Hot-file claims** use the same mechanism with the name `FILE_<path with / replaced by _>`, for example `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts`. See §4.\n- **Stale claims.** A claim older than 24 h whose PID is gone may be broken, but only by the owner, or by an agent that first records the broken claim in the log.\n- If the MCP Agent Mail server is available, its file reservations (`file_reservation_paths`) may be used as well. The git-common-dir claim stays authoritative, because every agent can see it without extra tooling.\n\n## 3. Isolation: worktree, branch, HOME, ports, processes\n\n| Resource | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Source | One `git worktree` per claimed slice: `git worktree add ../nexus-wt/<slice> -b v1/<plan>-<slice> origin/main`. Never edit `~/nexus-code` itself. It is the owner's checkout, and `/usr/local/bin/nexus` runs from it. |\n| Branch | `v1/plan-NN-pr-Nx`. One branch per slice. Never commit to `main` directly. |\n| HOME and state | Until PLAN-08 PR-8a lands, every test, build and probe run sets `HOME=$(mktemp -d)` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=\"$HOME/.config\"`. After PR-8a, the test setup enforces this, but scripts and probes outside `npm test` must still set it. **Never** run anything that writes to the real `~/.nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. |\n| Test runs | Full suites run on a **snapshot copy** of the worktree (`git worktree add` at the commit under test, or `git archive`), never on the tree being edited. `tsx` reads `src/` live. |\n| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n| Ports | Mock servers bind `127.0.0.1:0` (an ephemeral port) and publish the chosen port to a file. Fixed ports are forbidden. |\n| Processes | Kill only PIDs you started, taken from `$!` or `ps -eo pid,args \\| grep -v grep`. **Never `pkill -f` or `killall`.** Long background jobs are stopped through the agent's own task controls. |\n| Inference | There is one inference host (DEC-16). Any run that uses the gateway takes the claim `GATEWAY` first. That includes eval runs, live checks and stripped-proxy runs. One gateway run at a time, which keeps eval timings valid and prevents overload. Small cloud checks (E5) take `CLOUD` to avoid duplicate spend. |\n| Credentials | Agents never read or copy `~/.config/nexus/credentials.json`, `config.env` values, or gateway tokens. The stripped proxy reads the token file itself. |\n\n## 4. Hot files: serialise, do not merge blindly\n\nThese files are touched by several plans. An agent editing one must hold its `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit, test, push, merge, then release.\n\n| File | Plans that touch it | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cli/src/agent.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 11 | Claim required. Keep each change local to the line ranges the plan names. Rebase immediately before merge and re-run that slice's tests on the rebased snapshot. |\n| `cli/src/index.ts` | 01, 03, 04, 06, 08, 10, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-06 PR-6b (unified launcher) lands first.** Other plans add commands and flags afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tools.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 06, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-02 PR-2b (signal plumbing) lands before PLAN-03 PR-3c (`safeFetch`)**. |\n| `cli/src/sidecar.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/doctor.ts` | 01, 03, 05, 06, 08 | Claim required. Each plan adds **new check functions** in separate blocks. Only PLAN-06 PR-6d changes existing checks. |\n| `cli/src/client.ts` | 03, 05, 08, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-03 PR-3a (env layering) lands before any PLAN-05 slice.** |\n| `cli/src/repl.ts` | 01, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/config.ts`, `security.ts`, `sandbox.ts`, `mcp.ts`, `plugins.ts` | 01, 03 | Claim required. PLAN-01 PR-1e and PLAN-03 must not run at the same time on `sandbox.ts` or `security.ts`. |\n| `cli/src/core/capabilities.ts` | 01, 02 | **PLAN-01 PR-1a (effect classes) lands first.** PLAN-02 PR-2c adds schema fields afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tester.ts` | 01, 11 | **PLAN-01 PR-1c lands before PLAN-11 PR-11b/11c.** |\n| `cli/src/core/state.ts` | 04, 08 | **PLAN-08 PR-8a (HOME isolation) lands before everything.** |\n| `core/src/runner.rs`, `core/src/main.rs` | 01, 02, 06 | Claim required. The protocol version number is owned by PLAN-02 PR-2a. PLAN-06 PR-6c only reads it. |\n| `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/{live_state,live_view}.rs` | 01, 02 | Claim required |\n| `package.json`, `cli/package.json`, `core/Cargo.toml`, lockfiles | 01, 06, 07, 08 | **Only one agent at a time.** Version fields are written only by `scripts/sync-version.mjs`. Dependency changes are separate commits that touch only the lockfile and manifest. |\n| `harness.sh`, `install.sh`, `scripts/package-linux.sh`, `.github/workflows/*` | 05, 06, 07, 09, C1 | Claim required. Workflow edits are separate commits. |\n| `cli/dist/**` | all, while tracked | Do not commit `cli/dist` from slices. PLAN-07 PR-7d untracks it (DEC-10). Until that merges, only the merging agent rebuilds and commits dist, in a final separate commit, after rebasing. |\n| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `quickstart.md` | 05, 09 and doc tasks in every plan | Edit only the sections your slice changes. Hold a short `FILE_` claim. |\n\n### Ledger updates (`docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md`)\n\nA slice changes only the rows for its own requirement IDs: the status, test-reference and evidence cells, in place. It never reorders, adds or removes rows, and never edits another slice's rows. New requirements discovered during work go to §6, not into the ledger. Because rows are independent lines, git merges these edits cleanly. If a conflict appears anyway, resolve it row by row, keeping both changes.\n\n## 5. Commit and merge discipline\n\n1. Before **every** commit, compare `git rev-parse HEAD` with the commit you last made. Then run `git log <yours>..HEAD` on the branch. If anything foreign appears, stop and reconcile. Never commit generated artifacts (evidence, dist, lockfiles) over a branch that moved (lessons 2026-10-13).\n2. Commits are scoped to the slice. Do not reformat or clean up files outside the boundary.\n3. Merge order follows the precedence rules in §4 and the ROADMAP milestone order. Within a milestone, the slice with fewer hot-file claims merges first.\n4. Rebase onto `origin/main` immediately before merging. Re-run the slice's targeted tests, plus `make test`, on a snapshot of the rebased commit. Merge only if both pass.\n5. Pushing, tagging, publishing and changing GitHub state (issues, milestones, releases) are **owner-approved actions** (DECISIONS X-list). Agents prepare them and do not perform them.\n6. Preserve unrelated user work: the untracked `nexus-continuity-validation/`, the stashes, and any dirty files you did not create. Never stash, reset, clean or check out over them.\n\n## 6. Coordination records\n\n| Record | Where | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Claims | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` | Who owns which slice or file right now |\n| Claim log | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims.log` (append-only, one line per event) | Claim, release, broken claim, boundary request |\n| Boundary requests and discovered requirements | The PR description, under the heading `Needs from other slices`. Mirrored as one line in the claim log. | So the owning slice's agent can pick it up. Never fixed silently out of scope. |\n| Slice evidence | `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/<plan>-<slice>/` (new directory per slice) | No shared evidence files, so agents cannot overwrite each other's evidence |\n\n## 7. Safe parallel lanes\n\nThe following groupings can run at the same time with no hot-file contention beyond §4 claims. They are listed by milestone.\n\n| Phase | Parallel lanes |\n|---|---|\n| M0 | (a) PLAN-08 PR-8a, which must merge first. Then in parallel: (b) PLAN-06 PR-6a, (c) PLAN-09 PR-9a. |\n| M1 | (a) PLAN-01 PR-1a → PR-1b → PR-1c (sequential, owns `capabilities.ts`, `agent.ts` dispatch). (b) PLAN-02 PR-2a (Rust only). (c) PLAN-03 PR-3a → PR-3b (config/egress). (d) PLAN-11 PR-11a (`parser.ts`) and PR-11b (`tester.ts`, after PR-1c). Lanes (b), (c) and (d) do not touch `capabilities.ts`. |\n| M2 (alongside M1) | (a) PLAN-07 PR-7a/7b/7c (workflows, scripts). (b) PLAN-06 PR-6b after PLAN-01 PR-1b (shares `index.ts` flags). |\n| M3 | (a) PLAN-04 (`agent.ts` turn, operation runner). (b) PLAN-05 (`client.ts`, provider modules). (c) PLAN-10 harness (`eval/`, no product files). (d) Held-out task authoring by the agent author, **outside the repository**: no access to Nexus source or the development set (DEC-05). |\n| M4 (alongside M3) | PLAN-08 PR-8b..8e. Coordinate with PLAN-04 on `state.ts` through `FILE_` claims. |\n| M5 | **Serial on `GATEWAY`.** The eval runner, the stripped-proxy runs and the independent reviewer's live task each take the `GATEWAY` claim. The terminal matrix (E3) and the install matrix (E2) can run in parallel with gateway runs. |\n\n## 8. Independence constraints\n\n- The held-out task author must not see Nexus source, the development tasks or tuning history. It runs in a separate session with only the task schema and external repositories. Its output is sealed by a sha256 manifest committed before RC1.\n- The independent reviewer in ACCEPTANCE §8 must not be the agent or session that implemented the slices under review.\n- An implementing agent never edits `eval/heldout-manifest.json`, scorer thresholds or ACCEPTANCE §6.4 after the freeze.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1717f-73da-7b13-b06c-cebce8fdbefc","create_time":1792905671.2691739}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
