{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T08:16:55.399Z","ordinal":331503,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a17222-5667-70a3-9442-876dd12a2884","author":"/root/t4_runner_independent_review","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/t4_runner_independent_review\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** — the grace-period and active-turn fixes look sound by inspection, and the scorer now requires successful resume evidence. `git diff --check` passes; I did not run suites.\n\nThe SIGTERM gate appears unable to accept a real runner result: [`eval/run.mjs`](/home/mike/nexus-wt/plan10-pr10c-t4/eval/run.mjs:685) records `firstExitSignal` from the Node child’s `close` event, but that child is the `systemd-run` wrapper created by [`eval/resources.mjs`](/home/mike/nexus-wt/plan10-pr10c-t4/eval/resources.mjs:116). The payload receives SIGTERM inside the cgroup; `systemd-run` normally reports the payload’s signal-derived exit status and exits itself normally, leaving this `exitSignal` null. Yet [`eval/score.mjs`](/home/mike/nexus-wt/plan10-pr10c-t4/eval/score.mjs:143) requires it to equal `\"SIGTERM\"`.\n\nUse the supervisor’s final payload signal (`final.signal`) or validate the wrapper’s signal-derived exit code, and add a regression case matching the evidence the actual runner emits. The current synthetic fixture hardcodes `\"SIGTERM\"`, so it does not catch this mismatch."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17211-7ad4-71c1-9c02-690a797dca89","create_time":1792916215.399267}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":16592}}
